From: EglantinePS5 <noreply@eglantineps5.local>
Subject: [PATCH] EDEN_UP_HITCH_TRACE=1: name what the GPU thread spends a long frame on (buffer and image uploads, staging buffers and images made, decodes queued, collector passes, Finish waits); off by default

Ocarina of Time, the village after Link's door (batch t87-a, 10 Nov, runs 739-743): every run
has one frame of about 1.0 s on the GPU thread (heap.log EDEN_VULKAN_FRAME worst_ms 998-1029)
with no log line inside it: in run739 nothing between 87.745 s (a guest service call) and
88.752 s (the first 1365x1365x90 ASTC array made). It is 47% of the ~1.3 s a run loses to
hitch windows. The 5 s and 600-present lines give totals, not frames, and the pieces that could
take a second log nothing of their own time: in that second the buffer cache went from 24
buffers (1 MiB) to 998 (512 MiB) and asked the staging pool for 128 MiB and then 64 MiB with
nothing that large in it (stderr MKW_STAGING_MISS: a fresh allocation and mapping each), the
texture cache uploaded 619 new images in 23 frames and queued the two 90-layer arrays (an
unswizzle and a zero fill on the GPU thread each), and from 82 s (run742) the collector ran
in pressure mode with a Finish per downloaded image.

EDEN_UP_HITCH_TRACE=1 (Common::PeSwitch, exactly "1"; unset: every site is one bool check)
times each of these on the thread that runs it, in video_core/hitch_trace.h:

  image upload        TextureCache::RefreshContents, with the image's guest bytes
  image made          TextureCache::InsertImage (vkCreateImage + memory)
  decode queued       TextureCache::QueueAsyncDecode (unswizzle + zero fill, GPU thread part)
  decode landing      TextureCache::TickAsyncDecode
  collector pass      the RunGarbageCollector call in TickFrame (the function's own text is
                      left as it is: the PS5 prepare script rewrites it from its original)
  buffer upload       BufferCache::MappedUploadMemory (staging request, guest read, copy)
  buffer made         BufferCache::JoinBuffer
  staging buffer made StagingBufferPool::CreateStagingBuffer, to its mapping
  Finish wait         Scheduler::Finish

Two kinds of line, both HW.GPU Info:

  Hitch trace: buffer upload: 128.0 MiB in 1 copies at 0x7fc1800000 took 412.3 ms
      one operation of 20 ms or more (a staging buffer made: 5 ms or more).
  Hitch trace: frame 3081 took 1004.2 ms on the GPU thread; traced 980.1 ms | buffer uploads
  520.0 ms (3, 192.0 MiB, worst 412.3 ms: 128.0 MiB in 1 copies at 0x7fc1800000) | image
  uploads 301.2 ms (142, 388.5 MiB, worst 61.0 ms: 1365x1365x90 (11 levels, format 71) ...) | ...
      a frame (TextureCache::TickFrame to the next) of 100 ms or more: every kind with a
      count, longest first, with its count, bytes and worst operation. Traced is the frame's
      time inside the outer kinds; the rest is draws, command parsing and whatever has no Op.
      Finish waits, staging buffers made and decodes queued nest inside other kinds and are
      listed but not added to "traced".

The same operations are also zones of EDEN_NX2_GPU_PROFILE (image made, decode work,
collector, buffer upload, buffer made, staging made, finish wait), so with EDEN_NX2_GPU_PROFILE=1
and EDEN_NX2_SLOW_FRAME_MS=100 the profile's "Slow frame" line shows them next to draw,
update buffers and the rest. Zones are inert while the profile is unset.

Next run: EDEN_UP_HITCH_TRACE=1 on the walk-fast control; the frame line at the village load
names the ~1 s frame. The fix follows what it says: a buffer upload (upload the big dirty
buffers in pieces across frames, or keep a 128 MiB upload staging buffer from boot), image
uploads (spread the first-frame burst), or a decode queued (move the unswizzle to the worker).

